From b0617e5a43c24fc84733e95a3e54d55c414d8132 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 11:23:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall: complete the native rules engine and wire the hub
 up

Phase 1 of the native firewall suite. The Phase 0 page and the reaper_fw.c
foundation were carried inert since v2.3.3 (not in rc/Makefile, no nav
reference, no dictionary entries); this makes the feature real.

ENGINE (rc/reaper_fw.c, 360 -> ~1600 lines)
Object-model rules engine compiling reaper_fw_{obj,grp,svc,zone,zpol,rules}
into REAPER_FWI/FWF/FWO, dual-stack, ipset-backed. Address objects and groups
materialise as rwfw_/rwfw6_ sets; a geo object REFERENCES Warden's rw_g_<cc>
and is only emitted when Warden actually manages that country (S5). Rules
carry direction, zones, service, schedule, per-source rate and per-rule
logging, ordered by their order field. Zone policy is appended after the user
rules so an explicit rule always wins. The chain RETURNs at the end - this
layer ADDS policy on top of the stock firewall rather than replacing it.

Commit-confirm per the R1-R9 review: rollback REGENERATES from a /jffs
lastgood snapshot (never a whole-table restore, which would clobber
Gatekeeper/Warden/SDN), the deadline belongs to a durable cru watcher running
a PRE-GENERATED rollback script (so recovery needs neither httpd nor rc), the
armed token is a directory so mkdir/rmdir are the compare-and-set, and the
last resort is to remove our own jumps and leave the base firewall in charge.
Only reaper_fw_confirm() writes flash.

THREE CHANGES TO THE APPROVED PLAN, all load-bearing:

1. The S1 protective head now carries the RFC 4890 ICMPv6 set (errors 1-4,
   ND 133-137, MLD 130-132/143). Our chains are inserted ABOVE the base rules,
   and the base firewall's ICMPv6 accepts live in its own ICMP_V6 chain below
   us with no ND in allowed_icmpv6[] - so a single user DROP on the v6 side
   would have killed Neighbour Discovery, RA and PMTUD for the whole LAN.
   Commit-confirm would NOT have caught it: the operator's IPv4 session stays
   up, so they confirm a ruleset that has quietly broken IPv6. Echo 128/129 is
   deliberately left matchable so "block IPv6 ping" is still expressible.

2. xt_hashlimit is the only match this engine needs that is a MODULE on this
   kernel (=m; everything else is =y) and nothing else on the box loads it.
   The generated script probes it once and SKIPS any rate-limited rule when it
   is unavailable - never emits the rule without its qualifier, because
   dropping "-m hashlimit" from a "drop above 20/sec" rule silently turns it
   into an unconditional drop.

3. RFW_NAME_MAX 28 -> 25. "rwfw6_" is six characters and ipset's usable limit
   is 31, so a 26-28 character object name produced a set name ipset rejects
   at create, leaving rules pointing at a set that does not exist. Latent in
   the v2.3.3 foundation; no shipped image was affected because the generator
   did not exist yet.

CAUGHT BY GENERATING THE RULESET, NOT BY READING THE CODE
A host harness links the real generator against stubs and dumps the script for
a representative config. It immediately showed rfw_emit_rules() emitting EVERY
rule into EVERY chain-building pass: three copies in the owning chain, and on
the passes where the target chain did not exist yet the command simply failed.
Rules are now filtered to the chain being built (0 cross-chain leaks, 0
duplicate lines, verified). The same dump also showed a MAC object emitting an
ip6tables match against an rwfw6_ set that is never created (hash:mac has no
address family) - such rules are now IPv4-only and say so in syslog.

Adversarial S1-S6 pass then added: the admin anchor is re-seeded from
login_ip_str on every start, because /tmp is a tmpfs and commit-confirm does
not cover the boot path (a confirmed-but-restrictive ruleset used to come back
after a power cut with no exemption and no timer); stop_reaper_fw() takes the
same file_lock("firewall") as apply; and the set-sweep keep-list is sized past
its real maximum so it can never truncate and try to destroy a live set.

SURFACE
- httpd: reaper_fw.cgi gains cfg/save/opts/dryrun/preview/apply/confirm/
  rollback. Drafts land in nvram RAM only. Saved lists are bounded and
  restricted to printable ASCII. Existing http_id CSRF gate covers all of it.
- Reaper_Firewall.asp gains Rules, Objects and Zones tabs in the page's
  existing idiom, a compile-preview that runs the SAME generator the apply
  uses, and a commit-confirm countdown. Its xhr() helper gained the standing
  rule 34 one-shot guard - unfixed, the countdown poller would have forked its
  chain on every failed request.
- Nav: single menuTree entry (the shell cannot drive an in-page tab strip),
  reaper_native[], both rails, and a SUP{} entry redirecting the four stock
  filter pages to the hub. Stock pages stay byte-pristine: restoring the four
  menuTree lines is the whole rollback.
- i18n: the page referenced 158 RFW_* tokens that existed in NO dictionary -
  it would have rendered raw placeholders. All 158 seeded English across all
  25 packs (6482 lines, lockstep); Reaper_Firewall.asp added to the
  reaper_langcheck gate's scan list.

VERIFIED WITHOUT A BUILD: gcc -Wall -Wextra -Wformat=2 clean (negative-control
checked); reaper_langcheck 0 breakers with the new page in scope; 25 inline
script blocks parse under node in all 25 languages; ASCII-only; generated
ruleset inspected line by line against S1-S6.

reaper_fw_enable defaults to 0, so nothing changes until it is switched on.
NOT COMPILED. METAL OWED - the firewall cannot be validated off-device, and
the rmcpd lab MCP needs to be stood up for it.
